@page "/markdown-document"

<PageTitle>Markdown document — Backlog UI Storybook</PageTitle>

@*
    Combined usage. Everything here is MarkdownView or MarkdownDocument with the
    options a real surface turns on at once — a way into the editor, a copy
    button, remarks in two layouts — so it follows the Markdown chapter, which is
    where each block and inline is shown on its own.
*@

<StoryPage Title="Markdown document"
           Summary="A section of markdown as a surface rather than as a rendering: read by default, editable on request, copyable as source, and annotated. Every option MarkdownDocument and the read view take, switched on together. The blocks and inlines themselves are the Markdown chapter; the editor on its own is Rich text editing."
           Guidelines="@Rules">

    <Story Name="Reading, then editing"
           Description="MarkdownDocument is the read view with a way into the editor. `@@bind-Value` is the markdown, `@@bind-Editing` is which mode it is in, and `Title` names the thing being read.

Editing hands the same string to the editor that the parser was reading, so a body never exists twice. The copy button is offered in read mode only — in edit mode the text is already selectable.

`OnBlur` is a flush, not an exit: the formatting buttons sit outside the textarea, so every reach for bold blurs it, and the mode stays where the author left it. **Done** is what ends an edit. Press **Edit**, type, then click a toolbar button or out of the box: the line below moves and the editor is still open."
           Code="@DocumentCode"
           Source="@_documentSource"
           SourceLabel="Value">
        <div class="sb-surface" data-testid="markdown-document">
            <MarkdownDocument @bind-Value="_documentSource"
                              @bind-Editing="_editing"
                              OnBlur="FlushDocument"
                              Title="release-checklist.md"
                              TestId="doc" />
        </div>
        <p class="sb-state" data-testid="markdown-document-mode">@(_editing ? "editing" : "reading")</p>
        <p class="sb-state" data-testid="markdown-document-flush">@DocumentFlushState</p>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Reading a knowledge chapter"
           Description="`RenderKnowledgeMetadata` says the body is a chapter of a knowledge folder, so the `meta` fence under its heading is read as the record it is and drawn beside that heading. Left unset — every entry body, every checklist — the same fence stays a fenced block, which is what it is anywhere else.

`KnowledgeFolder` is what turns the status from a word into a value out of that folder's list, so the pill becomes a picker. `KnowledgeHrefFor` resolves a reference to an address, which is what makes `related` a link rather than a button the host has to handle.

All three are read-mode only. Press **Edit**: the author of a chapter is looking at the fence itself, and the record has nothing to be folded into."
           Code="@ChapterCode"
           Source="@_chapterSource"
           SourceLabel="Value">
        <div class="sb-surface" data-testid="markdown-document-knowledge">
            <MarkdownDocument @bind-Value="_chapterSource"
                              @bind-Editing="_chapterEditing"
                              RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                              KnowledgeFolder="KnowledgeFolder.Tech"
                              KnowledgeHrefFor="@ChapterHref"
                              Title="shared.md"
                              TestId="chapter" />
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Copying the source out of a read view"
           Description="`AllowCopy` puts a copy button on a read view, and `Source` is what it copies — the markdown, not the rendering. Pass both: the view renders from `Blocks` and has no way back to the text otherwise.

`CopyLabel` names the button. What it reports is CopyButton's: the clipboard belongs to the browser and the browser may refuse, so the result is shown rather than assumed."
           Code="@CopyCode"
           Source="@CopySample">
        <div data-testid="markdown-copyable">
            <MarkdownView Blocks="@CopyBlocks" Source="@CopySample" AllowCopy="true" CopyLabel="Copy this section" />
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Commenting on a section"
           Description="`Comments` are anchored to a block index — not to a character range, which nothing survives — so the record is `MarkdownComment(Id, BlockIndex, Body, Author, When, Resolved)`. Re-anchoring when blocks move is the host's; the view renders comments against the blocks it is told and never guesses.

`OnAddComment` hands back the block index a reader asked at. The host answers with an empty-bodied comment, and that emptiness is the whole of the trick: the view opens a comment with nothing said yet straight into its own textarea, so a reader who pressed the affordance can start typing immediately rather than pressing Edit on the placeholder it left behind. `OnEditComment` hands back the whole record with the body already replaced, `OnResolveComment` hands back an id to toggle, and `OnDeleteComment` hands back an id to take away outright — including the fresh draft's own id, when Cancel is pressed before anything was typed into it.

Hover a block for the affordance; a block that already has comments keeps it visible."
           Code="@CommentCode"
           Source="@CommentSample">
        <div data-testid="markdown-commented">
            <MarkdownView Blocks="@CommentBlocks"
                          Comments="@_comments"
                          OnAddComment="AddComment"
                          OnEditComment="EditComment"
                          OnResolveComment="ResolveComment"
                          OnDeleteComment="DeleteComment" />
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="The same comments, in the margin"
           Description="`Layout=&quot;MarkdownCommentLayout.Margin&quot;` is the only difference: same blocks, same comments, same callbacks. Inline puts a remark next to what it is about and pushes the body apart; margin keeps the prose continuous and the remarks level with their blocks.

One markup, two layouts — the margin cell is always emitted and follows its block until the grid picks it up. Below the narrow breakpoint it falls back to inline on its own, so a caller does not have to notice. Narrow this window and watch it.

Editing works the same either way: a fresh remark opens straight into a box with Save and Cancel rather than committing on Enter, because a remark is prose and Enter has to stay a newline. The draft lives in the view until it is saved, so Cancel means something — for an existing remark it puts the wording back, and for a draft nobody has typed into yet it asks the host to drop it, through the same `OnDeleteComment` a saved remark's own Delete button uses."
           Code="@MarginCode"
           Source="@CommentSample">
        <div data-testid="markdown-commented-margin">
            <MarkdownView Blocks="@CommentBlocks"
                          Comments="@_marginComments"
                          Layout="MarkdownCommentLayout.Margin"
                          OnAddComment="AddMarginComment"
                          OnEditComment="EditMarginComment"
                          OnResolveComment="ResolveMarginComment"
                          OnDeleteComment="DeleteMarginComment" />
        </div>
    </Story>

</StoryPage>

@code {
    private static readonly DesignGuideline[] Rules =
    [
        new("content-editing.md#editing-model", "Why a body opens as a rendering, and what an edit is."),
        new("content-editing.md#raw-markdown-escape-hatch", "When the source itself is the surface."),
        new("interaction-guidelines.md#auto-save-no-save-buttons", "Why a flush is not an exit, and what the save state says."),
        new("design-principles.md#low-chrome-content-first", "How much of a document may be affordance."),
        new("accessibility.md#screen-reader--announcements", "What a mode change and a saved edit announce.")
    ];

    private const string CopySample = """
        # A section worth taking with you

        The button copies **this markdown**, not the words you can see — the
        source is what pastes back into another read view.

        - Including the list
        - And its markers
        """;

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<MdBlock> CopyBlocks = MarkdownPreview.ParseDocument(CopySample);

    private const string CommentSample = """
        # Sync runs through the cloud service

        The desktop head cannot hold a GitHub token safely, so it talks to the
        cloud service and only the cloud service talks to GitHub.

        - The token never reaches a box we do not control
        - Two heads writing the same file agree in one place

        > Superseded once the device-code flow lands.
        """;

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<MdBlock> CommentBlocks = MarkdownPreview.ParseDocument(CommentSample);

    private string _documentSource = """
        # Before tagging

        Everything here is read far more often than it is written, which is why
        this opens as a rendering rather than as a textarea.

        - [x] Tests green on main
        - [ ] Release notes written

        Press **Edit** to change it, and **Done** to read it again.
        """;

    private string _chapterSource = """
        # Shared Technologies

        ```meta
        status: adopted
        related: [".tech/technology-graph.md"]
        ```

        The fence above is this chapter's record, and the pill beside the heading
        is the status out of it. Press **Edit** to see the four lines it was
        written as.
        """;

    private bool _chapterEditing;

    /// <summary>A real host maps a reference to whatever route it serves the
    /// chapter on. The storybook serves no knowledge folder, so it points at the
    /// page that does show how the mapping is written.</summary>
    private static string? ChapterHref(KnowledgeReference reference) => "/markdown-references";

    private bool _editing;
    private int _documentFlushes;
    private string? _documentFlushed;

    /// <summary>A real host would write the body somewhere. The storybook writes
    /// it to a line under the editor, because the part worth watching is not
    /// where it went — it is that the flush happened and the editor is still
    /// open afterwards.</summary>
    private void FlushDocument()
    {
        _documentFlushes++;
        _documentFlushed = _documentSource;
    }

    private string DocumentFlushState
    {
        get
        {
            if (_documentFlushes == 0)
            {
                return "nothing flushed yet — edit the text, then click a toolbar button or out of the box";
            }

            var flushes = _documentFlushes == 1 ? "one flush" : $"{_documentFlushes} flushes";
            var drift = _documentFlushed == _documentSource
                ? "the saved copy matches the editor"
                : "with edits typed since the last one";

            return $"{flushes}, {drift}";
        }
    }

    private int _nextComment = 4;

    private IReadOnlyList<MarkdownComment> _comments =
    [
        new("c1", 1, "Worth saying which token — the PAT or the app installation?", "Reviewer", "2 hours ago"),
        new("c2", 2, "Both bullets are the same point stated twice.", "Reviewer", "yesterday", Resolved: true),
        // Anchored past the end: the block it was written against has gone.
        new("c3", 9, "This was against a paragraph that no longer exists.", "Reviewer", "last week")
    ];

    /// <summary>Empty, on purpose: the view reads that as a fresh draft and opens
    /// it straight into its own textarea, so the reader types where they pressed
    /// rather than into a placeholder they would have had to find Edit to
    /// reach.</summary>
    private void AddComment(int blockIndex)
    {
        var id = $"c{_nextComment++}";
        _comments = [.. _comments, new MarkdownComment(id, blockIndex, string.Empty, "You", "just now")];
    }

    private void ResolveComment(string id) =>
        _comments = [.. _comments.Select(c => c.Id == id ? c with { Resolved = !c.Resolved } : c)];

    /// <summary>The whole comment comes back, body already replaced — so a host
    /// storing more than the view shows can match it up.</summary>
    private void EditComment(MarkdownComment edited) =>
        _comments = [.. _comments.Select(c => c.Id == edited.Id ? edited : c)];

    /// <summary>Taken away outright — a saved remark's own Delete button, or a
    /// fresh draft Cancel is abandoning rather than saving.</summary>
    private void DeleteComment(string id) =>
        _comments = [.. _comments.Where(c => c.Id != id)];

    private IReadOnlyList<MarkdownComment> _marginComments =
    [
        new("m1", 1, "The same remarks, drawn beside the prose instead of through it.", "Reviewer", "2 hours ago"),
        new("m2", 2, "Level with the block it belongs to, so nothing is pushed apart.", "Reviewer", "yesterday"),
        new("m3", 3, "Resolved, and still here.", "Reviewer", "last week", Resolved: true)
    ];

    private int _nextMarginComment = 4;

    private void AddMarginComment(int blockIndex)
    {
        var id = $"m{_nextMarginComment++}";
        _marginComments = [.. _marginComments, new MarkdownComment(id, blockIndex, string.Empty, "You", "just now")];
    }

    private void EditMarginComment(MarkdownComment edited) =>
        _marginComments = [.. _marginComments.Select(c => c.Id == edited.Id ? edited : c)];

    private void ResolveMarginComment(string id) =>
        _marginComments = [.. _marginComments.Select(c => c.Id == id ? c with { Resolved = !c.Resolved } : c)];

    private void DeleteMarginComment(string id) =>
        _marginComments = [.. _marginComments.Where(c => c.Id != id)];

    private const string DocumentCode = """
        <MarkdownDocument @bind-Value="_source"
                          @bind-Editing="_editing"
                          OnBlur="Flush"
                          Title="notes.md" />

        @code {
            // Blur is a save, not an exit. The toolbar is outside the textarea, so
            // reaching for bold blurs it and the editor has to still be there.
            private void Flush() => Save(_source);
        }
        """;

    private const string ChapterCode = """
        @* The three knowledge signals a chapter needs, handed to the same
           component every other body is read through. Read mode only — the
           editor is the fence as it was written. *@
        <MarkdownDocument @bind-Value="_chapter"
                          RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                          KnowledgeFolder="KnowledgeFolder.Tech"
                          KnowledgeHrefFor="@Route"
                          Title="shared.md" />

        @code {
            // The host owns the mapping, because only it knows what it serves.
            private string? Route(KnowledgeReference reference) => $"/knowledge/{reference.Path}";
        }
        """;

    private const string CopyCode = """
        @* Source is only for the copy button — the view renders from Blocks. *@
        <MarkdownView Blocks="@blocks" Source="@markdown" AllowCopy="true" />
        """;

    private const string CommentCode = """
        <MarkdownView Blocks="@blocks"
                      Comments="@comments"
                      OnAddComment="Add"
                      OnResolveComment="Resolve"
                      OnDeleteComment="Delete" />

        @code {
            // Anchored to a block index. Re-anchoring when blocks move is the
            // host's — the view renders what it is told and never guesses.
            //
            // Empty, not a placeholder: an empty body is what tells the view
            // this is a fresh draft, so it opens straight into its own textarea
            // instead of a paragraph the reader has to press Edit to reach.
            private void Add(int blockIndex) =>
                _comments = [.. _comments, new MarkdownComment(NextId(), blockIndex, "", "You", "just now")];

            private void Delete(string id) => _comments = [.. _comments.Where(c => c.Id != id)];
        }
        """;

    private const string MarginCode = """
        <MarkdownView Blocks="@blocks"
                      Comments="@comments"
                      Layout="MarkdownCommentLayout.Margin"
                      OnAddComment="Add"
                      OnEditComment="Save"
                      OnResolveComment="Resolve"
                      OnDeleteComment="Delete" />

        @code {
            // The whole record comes back, body replaced.
            private void Save(MarkdownComment edited) =>
                _comments = [.. _comments.Select(c => c.Id == edited.Id ? edited : c)];

            // Reached from a remark's own Delete button, and from Cancel on a
            // draft nobody typed into — see the read view's own notes on that.
            private void Delete(string id) => _comments = [.. _comments.Where(c => c.Id != id)];
        }
        """;
}
